WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.150
Wallets, transfers, USSD and bills: doing it right every time.

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.130
This is the video for module 2 of Digital Payments and POS Basics.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.330
A transfer takes less than a minute, but most mistakes happen in that minute. So you will learn a routine.

4
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.040
You will also pay without internet, pay bills, and receive money safely.

5
00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:37.880
Wallet apps like Opay, PalmPay and Moniepoint must be run by licensed companies. For example, Joy, a hairdresser in Port Harcourt, found two apps with the same logo, and chose the one whose publisher matched the company.

6
00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:47.380
She found the licence statement, used her own number, set a strong PIN and saved support. No licence statement? Do not keep money there.

7
00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:59.960
For example, Grace in Abuja pays school fees of 45,000 naira. She types the account number from the fee slip and checks it in groups of 3, 3 and 4 digits.

8
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:04.880
The app shows the school's registered name. It matches the slip.

9
00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:12.850
She checks the screen says 45,000, not 4,500 or 450,000.

10
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.940
In the narration she types her son's name, class and 2nd term fees, so the bursar can match it.

11
00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:27.940
She reads the fee, checks her balance covers it, enters her PIN, and saves the receipt in a phone folder.

12
00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:37.160
A message saying a supplier or school has a new account is a common trick. Confirm by calling the number you already have.

13
00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:42.290
If you send to a wrong account, report it to your bank at once with the session ID.

14
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:54.060
USSD is a short code that starts with a star and ends with a hash. It works on any phone with signal, with no data. Get the code from your card or bank website.

15
00:01:55.310 --> 00:02:00.560
It only works with the SIM registered to your account. That is part of its security.

16
00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:12.580
For example, Mercy owes a farmer, Musa, 3,000 naira in a village with no data. Sessions time out quickly, so she writes his account number on paper first.

17
00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:23.470
The screen shows Musa's name. She reads it aloud, and he confirms. Then she enters her USSD PIN. Never dial a code a stranger sends you.

18
00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.120
Every bill follows one pattern: service, number, amount, check, approve, keep proof.

19
00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:35.780
For airtime, choose the network that matches the number, or it may go to a different number.

20
00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:48.620
For prepaid electricity, enter your meter number exactly and check the name shown. The 20-digit token only works on that meter. If it is rejected, retype it slowly first.

21
00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:59.510
For TV, enter the smartcard or IUC number and keep the decoder on. If a bill does not deliver, check your history first. Do not pay again.

22
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.790
Patience has 20,000 naira to spend on electricity this week.

23
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:14.550
The freezer uses most power, so she buys 12,000 for the shop. The name on screen matches, so she pays and saves the token.

24
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:21.120
Home gets the rest, 8,000. The name shown is her landlord's, as she expected.

25
00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:28.340
20,000 in total, with two receipts and two tokens saved in case a meter rejects one.

26
00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:38.910
For example, Aisha sells fabric in Ilorin. At closing, her notebook shows 6 transfer sales, 55,500 naira.

27
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:49.660
Her app history shows only 49,500. 6,000 is missing, from the woman who showed a screenshot when the stall was busy.

28
00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.500
Release goods only when the credit is in your own app history.

29
00:03:54.750 --> 00:04:04.820
Sometimes a caller says he sent you money by mistake, and asks you to send it back. Check your balance, not the SMS, and do not spend money you did not expect.

30
00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:17.020
Tell him to report it to his own bank. Report it to yours, and keep the reference. If the money is real, the banks will move it back. If the alert was fake, you have lost nothing.

31
00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:34.220
To remember. Check the licence before trusting a wallet. Check the name, the number and the zeros before your PIN. Use USSD when data runs out. Check bill numbers before paying. And release goods only when the money shows in your own history.

32
00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:45.420
Check one wallet app, send a practice transfer of 100 naira to a trusted friend, do a USSD balance check, pay one bill, and run one end-of-day check.

33
00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.710
Continue with the lessons below.
